tags: linux-arm64-any-page, linux/arm64

A Linux virtual machine on AArch64, 64-bit, little-endian. The software of the service
runs as Linux processes in a guest with its own kernel. It does not share the kernel of
the host.

The node guarantees only the instructions of Armv8.0-A. Software that needs a later
extension (for example SVE) can fail. Another level is another architecture, with
another formal. The node can run the guest natively or with an emulator: the
benchmarks of the node show the difference.

The setting guest-kernel of Container.Init selects the kernel (0e5751c026e543b2e8ab2eb06099daa1d1e5df47778f7787faab45cdf12fe3a8:68a47f3f2dd263abcf8c26232666aa913f308276005bcb842ccdce3c4f030390).
With no such setting, the node selects the kernel.

The software reads the page size when it runs, and its segments are aligned for the
largest page. So the node can boot a kernel with pages of 4, 16 or 64 KiB. Every node
that runs AArch64 can announce this architecture, with the one of its page size.

Who is bound: the node that runs the service. An architecture has no value.

